By the end of this operation, you will be able to:
1.Read raw captured traffic like a first responder: identify Ethernet, IP, and TCP/UDP header fields and
map them to Sport/Dport and SIP/DIP.
2.Reconstruct the 5-tuple (socket) behind a live connection and cross-check it against the OS's own
socket table.
3.Capture and interpret an ARP request/reply exchange, field by field — your baseline for spotting a
forged one later.
4.Carve out address space for a new building under time pressure using VLSM, and summarize routes
for the border router.
5.Use whois and a direct root-server query to attribute a suspicious external address and place it in
the global addressing hierarchy.
6.Run UMBRA's suspected move yourself — an ARP poisoning attack — inside the range, then detect it
and shut it down from the defender's side.
7.Design and defend the secure network topology for Launch Day.

Requirements:
1.3 virtual machines (preffered OS: ubuntu)
2.all 3 machines should be on a 'internal network' of the same name (name: intnet) on network adapter 1 (route all communications between the vir mac through this)
3.all 3 machines should have a NAT connectivity on network apdater 2 for downloading stuff (route all internet traffic through this)
